<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Silk Road</h1><p class="path-tagline">Follow silk, ideas and armies along the routes that bound Eurasia together.</p><p class="path-intro">For over fifteen centuries, overland routes across Central Asia carried silk westward and horses, glass and gold eastward, along with religions and technologies. No single empire owned the Silk Road; instead, a relay of states opened, taxed, protected and sometimes disrupted it. This path follows that relay from the Han missions of the second century BCE, through the Parthian middlemen and the cosmopolitan markets of Tang Chang'an, to the Mongol peace and the Timurid splendour of Samarkand.</p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/han-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liu-bang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 206 BCE – 220 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Han Dynasty</span><span class="path-step-why">Zhang Qian's missions and Emperor Wu's western campaigns opened the routes, and Han garrisons in the Tarim Basin kept them running.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road"><span class="path-step-ph">2</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Silk Road</span><span class="path-step-why">An overview of the network itself: its routes, its goods and the ideas, Buddhism among them, that travelled along it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/parthian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arsaces-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Profile · 247 BCE – 224 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Parthian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Astride key segments of the route, Parthia profited as the intermediary between Rome and Han China, which knew it as Anxi.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians"><span class="path-step-ph">4</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · People</span><span class="path-step-name">Sogdians</span><span class="path-step-why">The Central Asian merchant people whose caravans and partnerships carried much of the trade between steppe, oasis and city.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/tang-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-gaozu-of-tang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 618 CE – 907 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Tang Dynasty</span><span class="path-step-why">Tang Chang'an, with perhaps a million people, hosted Sogdian, Persian, Arab and Indian merchants in its two great markets.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Event · 751</span><span class="path-step-name">Battle of Talas</span><span class="path-step-why">In 751 Abbasid forces checked Tang expansion in Central Asia, an encounter traditionally linked with the westward spread of papermaking.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/mongol-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karakorum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1206 CE – 1368 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mongol Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Mongol conquest joined China to Europe, with merchants protected by the Pax Mongolica and envoys speeded by the yam relay system.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica"><span class="path-step-ph">8</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Pax Mongolica</span><span class="path-step-why">The idea behind the relatively safe passage of goods, envoys and travellers across Mongol-ruled Eurasia.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/timurid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kamal-ud-din-behzad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1363 CE – 1507 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Timurid Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Caravan trade between China, India and the Mediterranean enriched Samarkand, Bukhara and Herat, a late flowering of the overland routes.</span></span></a></li></ol>
